1) Starting
-----------

Open the Diversions directory and double-click !Pacman. It takes over
the whole screen (or opens in a window, if you chose that last time)
and shows its title. Its icon also appears on the right of the icon
bar, and stays there while you are back at the desktop.

On the title, move up and down with the arrow keys (or point with the
mouse) and press Return (or click) to choose:

  Play           start a game
  View source    read the program in !JsEdit (part 9)
  Desktop        leave the game; its icon stays on the icon bar

Escape on the title also goes back to the desktop.

Click SELECT on the icon bar icon to come back to the game. Click
MENU on it for:

  Info                  about !Pacman
  Full screen           play on the whole desktop screen
  Window                play in a desktop window, which you can move
                        and resize (click it so that it has the
                        keyboard)
  Browser full screen   make the browser itself full screen too
  View source           read the program in !JsEdit
  Open directory        open the application's directory
  Quit                  leave for good, and take the icon away

Your choice of Full screen or Window is remembered for next time.
Quitting, even in the middle of a game, leaves nothing behind.


2) Keys
-------

  Arrow keys, or W A S D     steer Pac-Man
  ' (apostrophe), / , Z, X   steer too: up, down, left and right
  Return, Space              choose in a menu
  P                          pause; press it again to go on
  Escape                     in a game, the pause menu; on the
                             title, back to the desktop
  F                          browser full screen on and off

A gamepad works too: its pad steers, A chooses, Start pauses and B
opens the pause menu.

Pac-Man keeps going in the direction he is facing until a wall stops
him. Press a direction before you reach a corner and he takes the
turn as soon as he can; the last direction you pressed is the one he
wants.

The pause menu has Continue, Restart (a new game), Title and Desktop.
If you click away from the game, or the browser tab is hidden, the
game pauses by itself.


3) The rules
------------

The maze is the one from the arcade: 244 dots, four of them big
energizers, a tunnel through each side (Pac-Man goes out of one
edge and comes in at the other) and a pen in the middle for the
ghosts.

  A dot is worth 10 points, an energizer 50.

The four ghosts are a little slower than Pac-Man, and Pac-Man slows
for a moment after every dot he eats, so keep moving and keep taking
the turns early. The ghosts are described in part 4.

If a ghost catches you (you are both in the same square of the maze),
everyone is put back at the start and play goes on, with the dots you
have eaten still gone and your score kept. In this version nothing
else happens: there are no lives yet, no level to finish and no
fruit, and the energizers only score.


4) The ghosts
-------------

Blinky, the red ghost, starts above the pen. The other three wait in
the pen, moving up and down, and come out one after another: Pinky
at once, Inky after 4 seconds and Clyde after 8 (a stand-in: later
the dots you eat will let them out). Each leaves by the door at the
top, then turns left.

At every junction a ghost looks at the squares it could go to next
and picks the one nearest its target. It never turns back on itself,
and in a few places near the top of the pen and the bottom of the
maze it may not turn upwards. So you can read where a ghost will
go, and lead it a dance. Each has its own idea of a target:

  Blinky (red)    goes straight for Pac-Man's square.
  Pinky (pink)    aims four squares ahead of Pac-Man. When Pac-Man
                  faces up she aims four up and four left too, as
                  the arcade ghost did by mistake.
  Inky (blue)     looks at the square two ahead of Pac-Man and
                  at Blinky; his target is as far beyond that
                  square as Blinky is on this side of it. Inky is
                  most dangerous when Blinky is close to you.
  Clyde (orange)  chases Pac-Man until he is within eight squares,
                  then loses his nerve and goes for his own corner.

The ghosts also take turns. They start in SCATTER: for 7 seconds
each heads for a corner outside the maze (Blinky top right, Pinky
top left, Inky bottom right, Clyde bottom left) and so circles round
it. Then they CHASE you with the targets above for 20 seconds, then
scatter for 7, chase for 20, scatter for 5, chase for 20, scatter
for 5, and after that chase for ever. Every time the mode changes
all the ghosts in the maze turn right round, which is your warning.
On later levels the chases get longer.


8) Where things are kept
------------------------

!Pacman remembers whether you play full screen or in a window, and
browser full screen, in Choices:Pacman, which on the hard disc is
$.!Boot.Choices.Pacman:

  Settings   your choices

It is a text file (JSON) that you can read in !Edit. Delete it to go
back to the standard settings; a damaged file is ignored.


9) Reading and changing the program
-----------------------------------

All of !Pacman is written in JavaScript, and every part of it is in
the application directory, with notes for readers at the top of each
file. To read it, choose View source on the title or the icon bar
menu: !JsEdit opens the directory in a directory view, and you can
double-click a file to read it. From the Filer, Shift-double-click
!Pacman to open the directory, then Shift-double-click a file to open
it in !JsEdit (a plain double-click would try to run it).

The files, and what each one does:

  !RunImage   where it starts: the icon bar icon, the screen or
              window, the keys, and the main loop, which moves the
              game on 60 times a second and draws each picture
  Dirs        up, left, down, right, as numbers
  MazeData    the maze, as a list of rows of text
  Theme       the title, the ghosts' names and all the colours
  Maze        walls, dots and energizers; which ways are open
  Mover       how many pixels something moves each frame
  Player      how Pac-Man moves and eats
  Ghost       a ghost: bobbing in the pen, leaving it, moving
              through the maze
  Modes       the scatter and chase clock
  Levels      how long each scatter and chase lasts
  Targets     where a ghost is heading and how it picks a way
  Play        one frame of play, in the arcade's order
  Game        the state of a game, and its clock
  MazeDraw    draws the walls
  Sprites     Pac-Man and the ghosts
  FruitArt    the fruit
  Hud         the score panel
  Render      draws a whole picture of the game
  Menu        the menus on the title and pause screens
  Title       the title screen
  Screens     which screen is showing, and what the keys do on it

The rules and the drawing are kept apart from the desktop: only
!RunImage talks to it. The parts the game shares with other games
(main loop, drawing, text, keys, saved files) are in GameLib; its
guide lists them.

Changing it

Before you change anything, make a copy to work on: drag !Pacman from
the Diversions directory into a directory of your own (on the hard
disc, say), and change the copy. The original stays as it was.

Here are two small changes to try. In your copy, open Theme in
!JsEdit. In the list of COLOURS near the top, the line

  pac: [255, 255, 0],

gives Pac-Man's colour as red, green and blue parts from 0 to 255:
change it to [255, 128, 0] for an orange Pac-Man. Or open Play and
find the line

  const PAC_PCT = 80, GHOST_PCT = 75;

These are the speeds of Pac-Man and the ghosts as a percentage of full
speed. Try 60 for the ghosts to make them easier to escape, or 90 to
make them a real danger.

To try the change, quit !Pacman if it is running (Quit on its icon
bar menu), then double-click your copy: the program is read afresh
each time it starts. If you make a mistake, an error box says which
file and line it is in: open that file, put it right and try again.


10) Credits
-----------

A personal, non-commercial tribute to Namco's 1980 arcade game.
Pac-Man is a trademark of Bandai Namco; this program is not connected
with them.

!Pacman is new code, written from descriptions of how the arcade game
behaves; it contains nothing from the original program or its data.
The pictures are drawn by the program itself; any sounds to come will
be made by GameLib's synthesiser, with tunes of our own.
